WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.843
A database keeps each table in a file of fixed-size pages.

2
00:00:06.843 --> 00:00:13.487
Rows come in different sizes: a ledger row takes 48 bytes, and one with a long note over 500.

3
00:00:13.487 --> 00:00:18.059
So how do we fit them into one page, and find one again quickly?

4
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:21.484
The lab's page holds 4,096 bytes.

5
00:00:21.484 --> 00:00:26.311
Our rows: A is 300 bytes, B is 200, and C is 400.

6
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:30.766
The simplest way packs them back to back from the start.

7
00:00:30.766 --> 00:00:33.128
A at byte 0, B at 300, C at 500.

8
00:00:33.128 --> 00:00:38.442
To find C, you read A's length and skip it, then B's length and skip it.

9
00:00:38.442 --> 00:00:39.844
Slow, but it works.

10
00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:45.264
Say an index, a lookup table elsewhere, notes that C starts at byte 500.

11
00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:48.861
Now delete B, and slide C down to close the gap.

12
00:00:48.861 --> 00:00:52.610
C now starts at 300, and byte 500 is the middle of C.

13
00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:57.348
The index now points, with full confidence, at the middle of a row.

14
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.734
Next idea: a small numbered list at the start of the page.

15
00:01:01.734 --> 00:01:04.238
Entry 2 holds C's offset and length.

16
00:01:04.238 --> 00:01:09.176
Finding C is one lookup, and C can move, as long as entry 2 is updated.

17
00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:10.994
But how long is the list?

18
00:01:10.994 --> 00:01:13.916
Fix its size, and you cap the rows per page.

19
00:01:13.916 --> 00:01:16.905
Grow it, and every row behind it has to move.

20
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.378
A filing drawer shows the fix.

21
00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:24.725
Inside its front, a numbered list is taped: line 2 says where folder 2 sits.

22
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.521
Pull a folder out and push the rest together, and you correct the positions on the list.

23
00:01:30.521 --> 00:01:32.245
But line 2 is still line 2.

24
00:01:32.245 --> 00:01:36.205
Anyone who wrote down drawer 0, line 2 still finds the folder.

25
00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:37.306
Let's map it.

26
00:01:37.306 --> 00:01:38.968
The drawer is the page.

27
00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:42.075
The list is an array of slots, one per row.

28
00:01:42.075 --> 00:01:43.881
And each folder is a row.

29
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.757
So let the two grow toward each other.

30
00:01:46.757 --> 00:01:49.919
A 4-byte header at the start keeps two numbers.

31
00:01:49.919 --> 00:01:53.687
lower is where the slots end: 4, right after the header.

32
00:01:53.687 --> 00:01:57.590
upper is where the rows begin: 4,096, the end of the page.

33
00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.743
Everything between them is free.

34
00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:01.965
Insert A, 300 bytes.

35
00:02:01.965 --> 00:02:06.535
It goes at the back, so upper drops to 3,796.

36
00:02:06.535 --> 00:02:16.081
Slot 0 goes at the front and holds A's offset and length, 3,796 and 300, and lower grows to 8.

37
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:21.649
B, 200 bytes, gets slot 1, at 3,596.

38
00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:23.142
Now C, 400 bytes.

39
00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:26.745
Where does it start, and which slot does it get?

40
00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:27.945
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.898
At 3,196, in slot 2.

42
00:02:32.898 --> 00:02:39.689
lower is 16 now, and the free space is still one gap in the middle, 3,180 bytes.

43
00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:43.934
So the index stores C's address as page 7, slot 2.

44
00:02:43.934 --> 00:02:46.651
It never stores the byte offset.

45
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:47.824
Delete B.

46
00:02:47.824 --> 00:02:52.129
The page only marks slot 1 dead, as zero, zero.

47
00:02:52.129 --> 00:03:00.097
B's 200 bytes are still there, a hole between A and C, and the free gap is still 3,180.

48
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:04.669
Tidying the page slides the live rows toward the end.

49
00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:09.863
C moves up 200 bytes, to 3,396, and slot 2 gets the new offset.

50
00:03:09.863 --> 00:03:16.211
The index still says slot 2, so it still finds C, and the gap grows to 3,380.

51
00:03:16.211 --> 00:03:20.828
Same as the drawer: the folder moved, and line 2 didn't.

52
00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:23.122
Here the drawer picture breaks.

53
00:03:23.122 --> 00:03:26.970
A list taped inside a drawer takes no room from the folders.

54
00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.433
On a page, the slots and the rows share the same 4 KB.

55
00:03:30.433 --> 00:03:33.640
The page is full when lower and upper would cross.

56
00:03:33.833 --> 00:03:35.234
Insert D, 250 bytes.

57
00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:37.756
It gets slot 3, not the dead slot 1.

58
00:03:37.756 --> 00:03:43.500
An old index entry might still say slot 1, and it would find D instead of nothing.

59
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.964
It's like giving out an old phone number: whoever saved it reaches a stranger.

60
00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:56.991
PostgreSQL reuses a dead slot only after its cleanup, VACUUM, has removed every index entry that points at it.

61
00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.966
This layout is called a slotted page.

62
00:03:59.966 --> 00:04:05.726
PostgreSQL uses it, with a 24-byte header and 4-byte slots it calls line pointers.

63
00:04:05.726 --> 00:04:09.589
Its header calls the two numbers pd_lower and pd_upper.

64
00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:25.525
On a real 8 KB page of the ledger table, lower is 652 and upper is 656: a 24-byte header and 157 line pointers, 4 free bytes, then 157 rows of 48 bytes.

65
00:04:25.525 --> 00:04:29.983
One more row needs 52, so the page is full.

66
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:36.743
The slots add one step to every lookup: the index points at the slot, and the slot points at the row.

67
00:04:36.743 --> 00:04:38.465
That step buys one promise.

68
00:04:38.465 --> 00:04:43.568
A row's slot number never changes while the row lives, even when its bytes move.

69
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.164
One quick check.

70
00:04:45.164 --> 00:04:46.109
Delete C now.

71
00:04:46.109 --> 00:04:49.455
What happens to lower, upper and the free gap?

72
00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:50.619
Pause and think.

73
00:04:53.833 --> 00:04:54.765
Nothing, yet.

74
00:04:54.765 --> 00:05:00.929
Slot 2 is marked dead, and C's 400 bytes stay where they are until the page is tidied.
